  ],
  "text": "Electrons drift through a wire slower than you walk. The bulb still lights the instant you flip the switch. You were taught it works like water in a pipe. That is wrong in one important way. An electron in a household wire moves a few millimetres a second. At that speed it would take hours to get from the switch to the bulb. Think of a bicycle chain instead. Push one link and the whole loop moves at once. Every electron is already there, all along the wire, waiting. The push travels down the line at close to the speed of light. The electrons barely stir. And the energy is not even inside the wire. It rides in the field around it. The wire is a guide. It tells the energy where to go and where to be delivered. Nothing raced from your switch to your bulb. The whole loop simply started moving together. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
